1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,895
Your kettle goes quiet right before it boils. That's your signal it's almost ready.

2
00:00:06,065 --> 00:00:11,468
That loud roar isn't boiling. It's thousands of tiny bubbles collapsing.

3
00:00:11,638 --> 00:00:15,819
The heating element sits at the bottom, so the bottom water heats first.

4
00:00:15,989 --> 00:00:20,802
Right on the hot metal, water flashes into tiny bubbles of steam.

5
00:00:20,972 --> 00:00:26,884
But the water higher up is still below one hundred Celsius, two hundred twelve Fahrenheit.

6
00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:32,574
As a bubble rises into that cooler water, its steam turns back into liquid.

7
00:00:32,744 --> 00:00:38,350
The bubble vanishes in a split second, and the water slams shut with a tiny crack.

8
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:42,723
Thousands of those cracks every second make the roar you hear.

9
00:00:42,893 --> 00:00:47,790
Then the heat spreads, until the whole kettle reaches one hundred Celsius.

10
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:54,355
Now nothing cools the bubbles. They rise all the way up and pop softly at the top.

11
00:00:54,525 --> 00:00:59,568
The cracking stops, the noise drops, and the rolling boil begins.

12
00:00:59,738 --> 00:01:05,318
So a loud kettle is still heating. A quiet one is hot all the way up.

13
00:01:05,488 --> 00:01:11,378
Cogwise: how things work. Follow for the next one!
